# DocFlow

![CI](https://github.com/peko11-jpg/docflow/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)

Document & asset management system. Attach an NFC sticker to a physical folder or piece of equipment, and scanning it with a phone opens a page listing every current document for that item.

```
NFC tag → phone opens URL → Django looks up the asset by slug → PostgreSQL → document list
```

## Features

- **Assets and NFC tags**: each asset (folder, equipment) has a unique slug; the tag stores the URL `/asset/<slug>/`
- **Authentication**: login required for pages, API and file downloads; `Profile` model with `worker` / `admin` roles, created automatically for every new user
- **Document versioning**: uploading a document with the same title on the same asset creates a new version and archives the old one; only current versions are shown
- **Audit log**: create / update / delete of assets and documents is recorded, including the acting user (via middleware)
- **Protected downloads**: files are never served directly; a login-protected view streams them
- **REST API** (Django REST Framework): `/api/assets/` and `/api/documents/` with pagination and search (`?search=`)
- **Background tasks**: Celery + Redis; a task is queued when a new document is uploaded (currently a simulated notification)
- **Tests and CI**: 10 automated tests, run on every push with GitHub Actions
- **Docker**: development and production Compose setups

## Tech stack

Python 3.13, Django 6.1, Django REST Framework, PostgreSQL, Redis, Celery, Gunicorn, WhiteNoise, Docker Compose, GitHub Actions.

## Quick start (development)

1. Clone the repo and create a `.env` file in the project root:

```
SECRET_KEY=change-me
DB_PASSWORD=change-me
DEBUG=True
ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost,127.0.0.1
```

2. Start the containers and set up the database:

```
docker compose up --build
docker compose exec web python manage.py migrate
docker compose exec web python manage.py createsuperuser
```

3. Open http://localhost:8000/admin/, then:
   - create an **Asset**
   - create an **NFC tag** for it with a slug, e.g. `compressor-1`
   - upload a **Document** to the asset
4. Visit http://localhost:8000/asset/compressor-1/ (login required).

## NFC setup

Write the URL `https://<your-host>/asset/<slug>/` to an NFC sticker with any NFC writer app (for example NFC Tools on Android). Scanning the sticker with a phone opens the page. The phone must be able to reach the server (same Wi-Fi network in development).

## API

| Endpoint | Description |
|---|---|
| `GET /api/assets/` | List assets with their documents (paginated, `?search=`) |
| `GET /api/documents/` | List documents (paginated, `?search=`) |
| `GET /download/<id>/` | Download a document (login required) |

All API endpoints require authentication.

## Tests

```
docker compose exec web python manage.py test
```

## Production setup

```
cp .env.prod.example .env.prod        # fill in SECRET_KEY and DB_PASSWORD
docker compose --env-file .env.prod -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d --build
docker compose --env-file .env.prod -f docker-compose.prod.yml exec web python manage.py createsuperuser
```

The production setup runs Gunicorn, applies migrations and collects static files on start, and keeps the database and uploaded files in persistent volumes. Set `USE_HTTPS=True` when serving behind an HTTPS reverse proxy.

## Roadmap

- Fine-grained, role-based permissions
- Reverse proxy with HTTPS
- OCR for uploaded documents (full-text search)
- Real notifications (email / push) from the Celery task